<!-- [EN Sync v7.0] -->
<div class="lf-trap-card" data-trap="T1" data-hq-id="LF-T1-AI">
            <div class="lf-trap-header">
                <span class="lf-trap-badge">T1</span>
                <span class="lf-trap-diff">⭐⭐</span>
                <span class="lf-trap-id">LF-T1-AI</span>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="lf-trap-q">Which of the following figures is a symmetrical figure?
A. Parallelogram  B. Rectangle  C. Rhombus  D. Triangle</div>
                <div class="lf-trap-wrong">
                    <span class="lf-ex-label">Common Mistake</span>
                    <span >Common wrong answer: C. Rhombus. Students mistakenly think a rhombus has only one axis of symmetry, or confuse the concept of "equal diagonals" with "symmetry," believing all rhombuses are symmetrical figures. However, in reality, only squares and some rhombuses (such as squares) are symmetrical figures; a general rhombus (non-square) has no axis of symmetry.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="lf-trap-right">
                    <span class="lf-ex-label">Correct Approach</span>
                    <span >Correct answer: B. Rectangle. A rectangle has two axes of symmetry (horizontal and vertical lines through the midpoint), while a general rhombus (non-square) has no axis of symmetry. Parallelograms and triangles (except isosceles/equilateral) are also not symmetrical figures.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="lf-trap-note">💡 The axis of symmetry must allow folding to match; the diagonals of a rhombus are not necessarily symmetrical!</div>
            </div>
        </div>
<div class="lf-trap-card" data-trap="T2" data-hq-id="LF-T2-AI">
            <div class="lf-trap-header">
                <span class="lf-trap-badge">T2</span>
                <span class="lf-trap-diff">⭐⭐⭐</span>
                <span class="lf-trap-id">LF-T2-AI</span>
            </div>
            <div>
                <div class="lf-trap-q">The figure below is a square with a straight line passing through it. Is this straight line an axis of symmetry of the square?
(Diagram: a square with a diagonal line drawn from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner)</div>
                <div class="lf-trap-wrong">
                    <span class="lf-ex-label">Common Mistake</span>
                    <span >Common wrong answer: Yes. Students see the diagonal dividing the square into two triangles and intuitively think it is an axis of symmetry, but they overlook that the axis of symmetry must make both sides completely overlap. When folding along the diagonal of a square, although both sides are triangles, they are oriented in opposite directions and do not completely coincide (unless in the special case of a rotated square). In fact, the axes of symmetry of a square are the horizontal midline, vertical midline, and two diagonals. However, for the diagonal to serve as an axis of symmetry, it must be the square itself. Here, the "diagonal" is just a line, and students misunderstand the definition of "axis of symmetry."</span>
                </div>
                <div class="lf-trap-right">
                    <span class="lf-ex-label">Correct Approach</span>
                    <span >Correct answer: No. A square has 4 axes of symmetry: the horizontal midline, vertical midline, and two diagonals. But note: the diagonal is an axis of symmetry because folding along the diagonal makes the two sides of the square completely coincide (two right triangles). So this question is actually a trap within a trap: the correct answer is "Yes," but students think it is "No"? Wait, let me re-examine: folding a square along its diagonal indeed makes both sides completely coincide, so the diagonal is an axis of symmetry. The common mistake is that students think it is not, because they believe axes of symmetry must be vertical or horizontal. Therefore, the correct answer is "Yes," explanation: the diagonal of a square is an axis of symmetry; folding along it makes both sides completely coincide.</span>
                </div>
                <div class="lf-trap-note">💡 Try folding along the diagonal; don't forget the four axes of a square!</div>
            </div>
        </div>
